WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.640
As data centers race to meet the exponential growth of

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.280
AI workloads in AI factories powered in high megawatts and

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.140
even gigawatts, electrical demands per rack are surging to levels

4
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.520
once considered unthinkable to power much denser compute infrastructure.

5
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.560
The industry is now preparing for a new benchmark, the

6
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.280
one megawatt rack. ORv3 plays a central role in this

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.160
transition. It provides a standardized foundation that can be extended

8
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.340
to meet the needs of one megawatt rack deployments.

9
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.160
The key is to retain the mechanical and interface consistency

10
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.840
of ORv3 while allowing flexible enhancements that increase power and

11
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.580
thermal capabilities without forcing a complete redesign.

12
00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.080
From a power perspective, the journey to one megawatt involves

13
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.460
a few critical shifts.

14
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.480
First, power must be delivered at higher voltages and through

15
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.100
larger conductors, often in the form of overhead busways instead

16
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.880
of traditional floor PDUs.

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.440
These busways connect to high -capacity rectifiers, which then distribute

18
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.040
power down through the racks via new high -current busbar

19
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.380
extensions. At the rack level, internal distribution must be robust

20
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.820
enough to handle up to 100 kilowatts or more per

21
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.600
zone. Power shelves must support greater density and redundancy and

22
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.760
racks must allow multiple feeds to deliver the necessary amperage

23
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.620
safely. Throughout this architecture, grounding becomes a critical

24
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.060
design point with careful attention paid to bonding paths, fault

25
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.620
protection, and compliance with global safety standards.

26
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.900
Microsoft, Meta, and Google have come together at the OCP

27
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.500
to contribute a new way to distribute power, and thus

28
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.740
enable the one megawatt IT rack by introducing a power

29
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.920
rack. Instead of data center electrical busways feeding power shelves

30
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.600
directly to the IT rack, taking up precious space that

31
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.920
cannot be used for IT servers, this new approach implements

32
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.720
a sidecar power rack.

33
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.600
The sidecar rack is also based on ORV3 and high

34
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.700
-voltage DC. This specification is named Mount Diablo, and it

35
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.200
provides for options at 800 volts monopole DC or plus

36
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.640
minus 400 volts, reusing the supply chain components from the

37
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.220
electric vehicle industry. It receives the power from the facility

38
00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.700
power distribution, and then feeds it to the IT rack.

39
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.060
Either directly to the bus bar pre -converted to 48

40
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.720
volts with a busbar clip connector, or an

41
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.320
intermediate converter can be placed in the IT rack, which

42
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.320
then feeds the bus bar at the lower voltage needed

43
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.040
for servers. There is additional cable distribution and connector

44
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.020
innovation required associated with the Diablo rack for safety and

45
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:19.560
efficiency. But scaling power alone isn't enough, a one megawatt

46
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.540
rack generates extreme heat.

47
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.560
That's why liquid cooling, already part of ORV3's vision, is

48
00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:33.100
now a must. Cold plate loops, rear door heat exchangers,

49
00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.400
and facility water integration are all being designed as native

50
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.180
features, not add -ons.

51
00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:45.900
Blindmate manifolds and front -access cooling interfaces make serviceability

52
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.500
and safety manageable even at scale.

53
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:54.900
Importantly, ORV3's open and modular architecture enables vendors

54
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.580
and operators to adopt these innovations incrementally.

55
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.340
A facility might start with 30 -kilowatt racks and gradually

56
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.420
evolve toward 100 -kilowatt or more, while staying within the

57
00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.520
same rack standard. This allows for global interoperability, faster

58
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.060
innovation, and shared learning across the OCP community.

59
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.780
The one megawatt rack is no longer a concept, it's

60
00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:25.560
a necessity. ORV3 ensures that as we scale toward this

61
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.640
future, we do so with standardization, safety, and sustainability

62
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.360
in mind.

